outputText:
{
  "concept": "In a Downs cell, electrolysis of molten NaCl drives Cl⁻ to the central anode (oxidation to Cl₂ gas) and Na⁺ to the surrounding cathode (reduction to liquid Na), with an iron screen keeping the two products from recombining so each can be collected separately.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Thick dark-gray outer cell casing drawn in cross-section (rectangular vessel with rounded inner cavity)",
    "Pale lavender/blue body of molten NaCl filling the cell, with a horizontal blue liquid-surface line near the top",
    "Central orange anode (+) column rising from the bottom center with a flared orange head near the top of the column",
    "Two dark blue-gray cathode (−) bars in the outer annular region, left and right of the screen, connected to horizontal feedthrough bars passing through the casing wall",
    "Cylindrical iron screen drawn as a curved cross-hatched mesh barrier surrounding the anode region",
    "Black funnel/hood above the anode that narrows into the vertical Cl₂ collector tube",
    "Pale green vertical Cl₂ collector tube bending right at the top into a horizontal duct",
    "Small pale-green gas bubbles rising from the anode head up through the funnel into the tube",
    "Black upward arrow inside the collector tube indicating gas flow",
    "Arrow and label 'Cl₂ outlet' at upper right",
    "Inlet pipe at the top of the cell with a small vertical stem, labeled 'Inlet for NaCl' with leader line to the right",
    "Right-side sodium riser channel and pale pink flask-shaped reservoir holding liquid Na, labeled 'Liquid Na metal'",
    "Pale pink liquid sodium layer resting above the molten salt level in the reservoir and a thin pink band inside the cell marking the sodium layer",
    "Downspout from the reservoir to the right with arrow, labeled 'Na outlet'",
    "Gray 'Voltage source' box at lower right with thick black wires running to the anode stem and to the right-hand cathode feedthrough",
    "Label with leader line: 'Molten NaCl' on the left",
    "Label with leader line: 'Iron screen' on the left",
    "Labels with leader lines at the bottom: 'Cathode (−)' and 'Anode (+)'",
    "Overlay panel (appears on interaction) showing the half-reaction equations: 2Cl⁻(l) → Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻ and 2Na⁺(l) + 2e⁻ → 2Na(l), plus the overall 2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)",
    "Animated ion markers: green Cl⁻ circles and purple Na⁺ circles drifting through the melt"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "electrolysisRun",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Run electrolysis",
      "teaches": "Charge carriers move continuously: Cl⁻ migrates inward to the anode and bubbles off as Cl₂ that escapes up the collector tube, while Na⁺ migrates outward to the cathode and the resulting liquid Na floats up and drains to the Na outlet.",
      "loop": true
    },
    {
      "id": "electrodeFocus",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Which half-reaction happens where — clicking the anode or the cathode highlights that compartment and displays its balanced half-equation with electron flow direction.",
      "teachesDetail": "Anode = oxidation of chloride; cathode = reduction of sodium ion."
    },
    {
      "id": "screenToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Iron screen",
      "teaches": "Without the screen, rising Na and Cl₂ meet and recombine violently; the screen is what makes separate, safe collection of the two products possible.",
      "options": [
        "Screen in place",
        "Screen removed"
      ],
      "default": "Screen in place"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The cell at rest",
      "narration": "A steel vessel holds molten NaCl above 800 °C. A central graphite anode sits inside a cylindrical iron screen; the ring-shaped cathode surrounds it. No current flows yet, so the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions drift randomly.",
      "state": {
        "electrolysisRun": false,
        "screenToggle": "Screen in place"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Switch on the voltage source",
      "narration": "With the external supply connected, ions stop drifting and start migrating: green Cl⁻ moves inward to the positive anode, purple Na⁺ moves outward to the negative cathode.",
      "state": {
        "electrolysisRun": true,
        "screenToggle": "Screen in place"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Oxidation at the anode",
      "narration": "Click the orange anode: each pair of chloride ions gives up electrons, 2Cl⁻(l) → Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻. Bubbles collect in the hood and rise through the green tube to the Cl₂ outlet.",
      "state": {
        "electrolysisRun": true,
        "screenToggle": "Screen in place"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reduction at the cathode",
      "narration": "Click the outer cathode bars: Na⁺(l) + e⁻ → Na(l). Molten sodium is less dense than the salt bath, so it floats to the top, fills the side reservoir, and drains out the Na outlet.",
      "state": {
        "electrolysisRun": true,
        "screenToggle": "Screen in place"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why the iron screen matters",
      "narration": "Remove the screen and the rising sodium meets the chlorine bubbles — the products recombine back into NaCl (and dangerously so). The screen keeps the two half-cell products physically apart, which is what makes the process continuous and useful.",
      "state": {
        "electrolysisRun": true,
        "screenToggle": "Screen removed"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Electrolysis only pays off industrially if the two products are kept apart — the Downs cell's geometry (central anode, surrounding cathode, iron screen, buoyant liquid Na, rising Cl₂ gas) is engineered entirely around separating sodium from chlorine the instant they form.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Centered cross-sectional schematic on white. Thick dark-gray double-walled vessel outline forms a squarish cell with a rounded inner cavity; interior filled pale lavender-blue (molten NaCl) with a horizontal medium-blue line marking the melt surface near the top. From the bottom center an orange column (anode, +) rises to about mid-height and ends in a wider orange flared head. A black funnel/hood sits just above that head and narrows into a pale yellow-green vertical tube that exits the top of the cell, bends right, and runs horizontally to a black arrow labeled 'Cl₂ outlet' at the upper right. Small pale green bubble dots drift from the anode head up into the funnel and tube; a long black arrow inside the tube shows gas direction. A curved cross-hatched mesh (iron screen) arcs around the anode region, drawn as a fine grid with slightly bowed top and bottom edges. Two dark slate-blue vertical bars flank the screen outside it (cathode, −), each joined to horizontal feedthrough bars that pierce the casing left and right. On the right, a channel leads up to a pale-pink flask/reservoir labeled 'Liquid Na metal', with a pink downspout exiting right to an arrow labeled 'Na outlet'; a thin pink band inside the cell shows the sodium layer floating on the melt. A small vertical stem on the cell lid is labeled 'Inlet for NaCl' by a leader line to the right. Lower right holds a gray 'Voltage source' box with thick black wires: one to the anode stem, one to the right cathode feedthrough. Left-side leader labels: 'Molten NaCl' and 'Iron screen'. Bottom leader labels: 'Cathode (−)' and 'Anode (+)'. Color coding for interaction: Cl⁻/Cl₂ in green, Na⁺/Na in pink-purple, anode highlight orange, cathode highlight blue. Half-reaction text appears in a light callout panel near the clicked electrode.",
  "reconstructionNotesExtra": "Animation should move ion markers along short radial paths (inward green, outward purple), spawn bubbles at the anode head, and gradually raise the pink level in the side reservoir with a small trickle at the Na outlet."
}

